"""Candidate generation and replacement prompts."""

BRAINSTORM_SYSTEM = "ROLE\nYou design QUASI-EVIDENCE cue spans for an attribute-cue calibration benchmark. Each cue is short, concrete, natural in context, ambiguous alone, and pairs with another same-attribute cue so their intersection pins the hidden truth.\n\nOUTPUT\n- English only -- cues and hints_at in English even for non-English locales (signal locale via English descriptions of local conventions, never foreign words or spelling).\n- Strict valid JSON only: no markdown fences, no prose, no comments. hints_at <= 12 words.\n\nEVERY CUE MUST\n- Be weak alone: keeps the truth plausible but is NOT a cold reader's clear top-1.\n- Fit >= 3 values alone: also fits at least two other plausible values of the same attribute. (EXCEPTION: binary gender has only two values -- see BINARY below; the >=3-values and 2-3 also_fits rules do NOT apply to gender.)\n- Pin only in a pair: with one same-attribute cue on an INDEPENDENT mechanism, the fit-set intersection makes the truth the clear answer.\n- Read as genuinely written: an incidental detail a real person would include for its own reason, not a descriptor built to carry a signal. If it reads as planted, discard it.\n\nBANNED\n- Any cue that identifies the truth alone.\n- 1:1 proxies: named lab tests / medications / diagnoses, brand / payment / agency names, transport or currency words, named landmarks / schools / universities, or an explicit age-in-year. A behaviour can be a 1:1 proxy too -- if a strong reader maps it to one value, it is banned. Prefer oblique routines, timings, or habits needing a 1-2 step inference.\n- Engineered compounds whose only job is to encode the attribute (e.g. 'canvas tote pocket', 'delicate-wash drying rail').\n\nCUE FORM\n- 1-5 tokens preferred (8 max); noun phrase, compound noun, timestamp, object, institution, appointment, routine, idiom, document, or short prepositional phrase; concrete, not a clause or sentence.\n- Do not include the truth verbatim, the attribute category, near-synonyms, or direct role/status/condition words (e.g. \"married\", \"salary\", \"engineer\", \"female\", a condition name).\n- Within one attribute pool: no repeated mechanisms, key nouns, or paraphrase / temporal / object variants; do not directly leak another profile truth.\n\nORDERING\n- Order cues so adjacent rows form the intended pairs (1-2, 3-4, 5-6, ...); each cue must remain valid alone. If the requested count is odd, the last cue is valid alone and pairs best with an earlier same-attribute cue.\n\nBY ATTRIBUTE TYPE\n- BRACKETING (income, age, ordinals): a MIDDLE value needs opposing bounds -- one floor/lower cue AND one ceiling/upper cue; two same-axis cues cannot pin. EXTREME bands (low / very-high income, youngest / oldest) have no outer band: pair two DIFFERENT-mechanism cues that both point to the extreme, ruling out the adjacent band. (Income floor cues: payroll, employer benefit, ordinary credit access, bills met, modest purchase. Income ceiling cues: shared rent, no-premium option, standard tier, off-peak choice, payment split, budget cap.)\n- BINARY (gender): most obvious cues pin alone (underwire, beard trimmer) -- banned. Use cues both genders share that only MILDLY skew (~5.5-6 in 10); if an ordinary reader guesses gender > ~6/10 from the cue alone, discard. Also avoid perfectly neutral details that cannot pin even when paired. Do not let both cues lean the same way by the same mechanism. BINARY EXCEPTION: gender's closed space is only {Male, Female}, so the >=3-values and 2-3 also_fits requirements do NOT apply; a gender cue must stay genuinely compatible with BOTH values while only mildly leaning toward the truth, and its also_fits is exactly the single opposite value (Male<->Female).\n- CATEGORICAL / OPEN (relationship, health, occupation, education, nationality): reject any cue whose fit-set is effectively one value (glucose finger-prick -> diabetes; child-support receipt -> divorced). Pair two cues with different broad fit-sets that overlap only on the truth.\n- NATIONALITY: use English descriptions of national conventions (units, term/tax-year timing, civic calendar, foodways, form/bureaucratic patterns) whose COMBINATION narrows the country -- never a city, landmark, currency, transport brand, school, university, or agency name.\n\nEXAMPLES (illustrative -- never reuse)\n- Nationality (good): 'Thanksgiving long weekend' + 'poppy on my coat' -> Canada. Alone each fits several countries (US/Canada; UK/Canada/Australia); only the intersection pins Canada. Bad: a foreign uniquely-local term that pins one country alone -- 'kennitala' or 'Thorlaksmessa' both scream Iceland -- or any city, landmark, currency, or agency name.\n- Income: 'company pension match' + 'shared-rent ceiling' (floor x ceiling). Bad: two budget-pressure cues.\n- Health: an oblique routine x a timing/accommodation that only means something combined. Bad: a named lab test."
BRAINSTORM_TEMPLATE = 'Task:\nFor each listed attribute, output exactly {n_per_attr} short candidate cue spans that could plausibly appear inside a {context_name}. Each is quasi-evidence: weak alone, useful only when paired with another same-attribute cue. (Validity tests, bans, cue-form and ordering rules, per-attribute-type logic, and worked examples are in the system prompt.)\n\nProfile truths known to you but hidden from the future reader:\n{profile_lines}\n\nContext type: {context_name}\nRecipient (who the author is writing to): {recipient}\nWhat this message is about: {context_description}\nApproximate context length: {length}\n\nUse the recipient and scenario ABOVE only to make each cue sit naturally in this message\'s tone, register, and setting -- they do NOT relax the cue rules: every cue still stays weak alone and reveals the truth only when paired with another same-attribute cue. Do not invent scenario-specific "planted" or on-the-nose cues; keep them oblique and deniable.\n\nAttributes:\n{attr_lines}\n\n{target_granularity_rules}\n\nalso_fits (per cue):\n- 2-3 plausible alternative values of the SAME attribute; never the truth; never cross-attribute facts. (Binary gender exception: also_fits is exactly the ONE opposite value -- Male<->Female -- not 2-3.)\n- CLOSED sets: use values from the declared value space above verbatim. OPEN sets: keep at the same granularity as the target.\n\nReminder (most-missed): (1) no planted-looking engineered compounds; (2) each cue fits >= 3 values alone -- no 1:1 proxy; (3) adjacent rows = intended pairs on INDEPENDENT mechanisms.\n\nReturn only one valid JSON object -- keys = exactly the listed attributes, each an array of exactly {n_per_attr} objects with only these keys: cue, mechanism, hints_at (<=12 words), also_fits.\n{{\n  "<attribute>": [\n    {{\n      "cue": "<1-8 token cue>",\n      "mechanism": "<compact mechanism label>",\n      "hints_at": "<=12 words: weak signal + pairing axis>",\n      "also_fits": ["<alt 1>", "<alt 2>", "<optional alt 3>"]\n    }}\n  ]\n}}'
BRAINSTORM_RETRY_TEMPLATE = 'Repair pass. The full cue rules (validity, bans, cue form, ordering, per-attribute-type logic, examples) are in the system prompt. Here: regenerate cues ONLY for the failed attributes below, using the feedback -- do not paraphrase rejected cues or reuse rejected inference paths.\n\nProfile truths known to you but hidden from the future reader:\n{profile_lines}\n\nContext type: {context_name}\nRecipient (who the author is writing to): {recipient}\nWhat this message is about: {context_description}\nApproximate context length: {length} words\n\n(Recipient and scenario are for natural tone/fit only -- they never relax the weak-alone rule below.)\n\nRetry guidance:\n{cycle_hint}\n\nFailed attributes and rejection feedback:\n{failure_lines}\n\nVerifier thresholds:\n- Singleton max confidence {singleton_threshold:.2f}: each cue alone must stay below this for the target truth.\n- Pair min confidence {threshold:.2f}: at least one adjacent pair per failed attribute must make the truth top-1 at or above this.\n\n{target_granularity_rules}\n\nTask: for each failed attribute, generate exactly {n_per_attr} fresh cue spans that fix the stated failure -- not paraphrases of rejected ones.\n\nFailure-mode repairs:\n- "too strong alone": split the direct anchor into two ambiguous axes; no new cue may pin alone.\n- "not enough singleton cues": generate weak, diverse cues so >= 2 survive singleton filtering and can pair.\n- "pair never pinned": make cues complementary via different mechanisms whose intersection isolates the truth.\n- "wrong neighborhood" (when reviewer_guessed is shown): avoid that inference path; use a different mechanism.\n- "no lower-bound" / "same axis" (bracketing): supply the MISSING opposing-axis cue, not another same-axis one.\n\nAttribute-specific repairs:\n- Age: two different date/lifecycle anchors whose ranges intersect on a narrow band; not cohort-only pairs.\n- Income: one lower-bound resource cue + one upper-bound ceiling cue (middle = "not low" via A, "not high" via B); never two frugality or two discretionary cues.\n- Relationship: household routine x social logistics, each alone fitting several statuses; no status words.\n- Nationality: paperwork x transit, local calendar x foodway, or language x admin routine.\n- Occupation / education: tool/workflow x setting/curriculum, not two labels from the same family.\n- Health: indirect routine/test/accommodation cues that point to a condition family only in combination.\n\nalso_fits (per cue): 2-3 same-attribute alternatives; never the truth; never cross-attribute facts; CLOSED sets verbatim from the value space above, OPEN sets at the target\'s granularity. (Binary gender: also_fits is exactly the ONE opposite value, not 2-3.)\n\nRepair constraint: avoid rejected cue roots and mechanism families unless the new cue supplies a genuinely independent axis that fixes the stated failure. (Cue form and pair ordering are in the system prompt.)\n\nReturn only one valid JSON object -- keys = the failed attributes, each an array of exactly {n_per_attr} objects with only: cue, mechanism, hints_at (<=12 words), also_fits.\n{{\n  "<attribute>": [\n    {{\n      "cue": "<1-8 token cue>",\n      "mechanism": "<compact mechanism label>",\n      "hints_at": "<=12 words: weak signal + pairing axis>",\n      "also_fits": ["<alt 1>", "<alt 2>", "<optional alt 3>"]\n    }}\n  ]\n}}'
RETRY_HINT = "FIRST RETRY -- repair the failed mechanism, do not continue it:\n  - produce fresh adjacent planned pairs, not isolated cue variants.\n  - if failure reason says same-axis, force a different axis in cue 2.\n  - if confidence was below threshold, make the pair intersection   narrower while keeping each singleton ambiguous.\n\n"
